* dsm.sys.template — IBM Storage Protect dsmadmc client options
* Template for the MCP Server deployment.
*
* Usage:
*   1. Copy to /opt/sp-mcp-server/config/dsm.sys
*   2. Replace placeholders:
*        your-sp-server  → hostname or IP of the IBM SP server
*        SP_SERVER_1     → a short label for this server (used with -SE= on dsmadmc CLI)
*   3. Set permissions:  chmod 600 /opt/sp-mcp-server/config/dsm.sys
*   4. Export the config path before starting the MCP server:
*        export DSM_CONFIG=/opt/sp-mcp-server/config/dsm.sys
*   5. Populate the password stash (one-time per account):
*        dsmadmc -id=mcp-svc-readonly -pa=<password> -se=SP_SERVER_1 "QUERY STATUS"
*      After this run dsmadmc reads the password from the encrypted stash
*      automatically — no -PA= argument is needed.
*
* NET-3 (impl-security-network.md): This file enforces SSL=Yes and
*   SSLREQUIRED=Yes so that dsmadmc refuses to connect without TLS,
*   independently of the SP account-level SESSIONSECURITY setting.
*
* See also: docs/implement/impl-security-network.md § NET-3

SERVERNAME          SP_SERVER_1

* ── Connection ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
* Replace 'your-sp-server' with the hostname or IP address of the IBM SP server.
TCPSERVERADDRESS    your-sp-server
TCPPORT             1500
COMMMETHOD          TCPIP

* ── TLS (NET-3) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
* SSL=Yes : use TLS for session authentication (default from IBM SP v8.1.2+).
*           Object data is sent over TCP unless SSL=Yes is set with the server
*           configured to encrypt all data (see IBM SP documentation).
* SSLREQUIRED=Yes : refuse the connection entirely if TLS negotiation fails.
*           This prevents silent fallback to unencrypted TCP.
SSL                 Yes
SSLREQUIRED         Yes

* ── Password storage (CRED-2) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
* PASSWORDACCESS GENERATE: dsmadmc stores the password in an OS-encrypted
*   stash file after the first interactive login.  Subsequent invocations
*   read the password from the stash — no -PA= argument is needed on the
*   command line and the password is never visible in /proc/<pid>/cmdline.
*
* To populate the stash (run once per account):
*   dsmadmc -id=<account> -pa=<password> -se=SP_SERVER_1 "QUERY STATUS"
*
* Once the stash is populated, set SP_MCP_USE_PASSWORD_STASH=1 in .env
*   to enable stash-mode in the MCP server's DsmAdmcWrapper.
PASSWORDACCESS      GENERATE


* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
* MULTI-SERVER DEPLOYMENTS
* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
* For environments with more than one IBM SP server, add one SERVERNAME
* stanza per server below.  Each stanza is independent; populate the stash
* separately for each server/account combination.
*
* Example:
*
* SERVERNAME          SP_SERVER_2
* TCPSERVERADDRESS    sp-server-02.example.com
* TCPPORT             1500
* COMMMETHOD          TCPIP
* SSL                 Yes
* SSLREQUIRED         Yes
* PASSWORDACCESS      GENERATE
